<GuideLayout
  title="Activewear Fabric Guide: Nylon, Polyester, GSM and Squat-Proof Testing"
  type="FABRIC GUIDE"
  description="Compare activewear fabrics by hand feel, compression, opacity, durability, cost and buyer use case before choosing fabric for leggings, sports bras and yoga sets."
>
  <h2>The Best Activewear Fabric Depends on the Product Goal</h2>
  <p>There is no single best fabric for every activewear product. A yoga legging needs soft stretch and opacity. A running sports bra needs recovery and support. A gym short may need abrasion resistance, quick drying and lower heat retention. Good sourcing starts by matching fabric performance to the product use case.</p>

  <p>For private label activewear brands, the most important fabric decisions are composition, GSM, knit structure, spandex percentage, stretch direction, opacity, hand feel and wash stability.</p>

  <h2>Nylon/Spandex vs Polyester/Spandex</h2>
  <p>Nylon and polyester can both work well for activewear, but they feel different and support different pricing strategies.</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Fabric Route</th>
        <th>Best For</th>
        <th>Buyer Advantage</th>
        <th>Watch Point</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Nylon/spandex</td>
        <td>Premium leggings, yoga sets, soft training apparel</td>
        <td>Soft hand feel, strong stretch, premium retail positioning</td>
        <td>Usually higher cost than polyester blends</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Polyester/spandex</td>
        <td>Gym wear, teamwear, printed styles, value collections</td>
        <td>Good durability, quick drying, more cost efficient</td>
        <td>Can feel less soft if yarn and finishing are not selected carefully</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Recycled nylon or polyester</td>
        <td>Sustainable activewear collections</td>
        <td>Supports lower-impact brand positioning</td>
        <td>Claims should be backed by fabric documentation and testing</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>How GSM Changes Fabric Performance</h2>
  <p>GSM measures fabric weight, but it does not guarantee quality by itself. A heavier fabric can improve coverage and compression, while a lighter fabric can improve drape and breathability. The right GSM depends on the product category.</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Product Type</th>
        <th>Typical GSM Direction</th>
        <th>Why It Matters</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Yoga leggings</td>
        <td>Medium to heavy</td>
        <td>Better squat-proof coverage and shaping support</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Sports bras</td>
        <td>Medium to heavy</td>
        <td>Improves support, hold and long-term recovery</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Tops and tanks</td>
        <td>Light to medium</td>
        <td>Improves comfort, breathability and layering</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Fabric Tests to Request Before Bulk Production</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Opacity test:</strong> check squat-proof coverage under stretch, especially for light colors.</li>
    <li><strong>Stretch recovery:</strong> pull the fabric and confirm it returns without bagging.</li>
    <li><strong>Pilling test:</strong> evaluate surface durability after friction.</li>
    <li><strong>Colorfastness:</strong> check washing, sweat and rubbing resistance.</li>
    <li><strong>Shrinkage:</strong> wash and measure before final size approval.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Fitclo Recommendation</h2>
  <p>If you are building a premium leggings or yoga set line, start with nylon/spandex samples in two or three GSM levels and compare them after washing. If you are building a cost-sensitive gym wear line, compare polyester/spandex and nylon/spandex side by side so you can decide whether the hand-feel upgrade supports the target retail price.</p>
</GuideLayout>
